---
title: Kung pao chicken
servings: 4
tags: [chinese, sichuan, chicken, stir-fry]
cuisine: chinese
time: 40 minutes
source: Kung Pao Chicken by Judy Leung, The Woks of Life, https://thewoksoflife.com/2015/02/kung-pao-chicken/
---

== Peanuts ==

Heat @vegetable oil{1%tsp} in a #wok{} over medium heat. Add the
@peanuts{1%cup}(raw, shelled) and stir constantly ~{3%minutes}. Turn off the heat
and keep stirring ~{1%minute} more, then let them cool until crunchy.

> Already-roasted peanuts can stand in; skip this step.

== Chicken ==

Toss the @chicken breast{12%oz}(cut into 3/4-inch cubes) with
@vegetable oil{1%tsp}, @cornstarch{1%tsp}, @Shaoxing wine{1%tsp},
@kosher salt{1/8%tsp} and a pinch of @ground white pepper{}. Leave
~{20%minutes}.

== Sauce ==

Stir together the @light soy sauce{1%tbsp}, @dark soy sauce{1/2%tsp},
@rice vinegar{1%tbsp}, @sugar{1%tsp}, @water{3%tbsp} and @cornstarch{1%tsp}.

== Stir-fry ==

Heat 2 tbsp of the @vegetable oil{3%tbsp} in the wok over high heat and sear
the chicken until browned and just cooked. Lift it into a bowl.

Turn the heat to low and add the last 1 tbsp of oil, the
@garlic{3%cloves}(smashed and sliced), @ginger{2%slices}(thin, minced),
@dried chilies{2}(red, seeded, chopped), @Sichuan peppercorns{1/2%tsp}(ground)
and the @scallions{6}(white parts only, cut into 3/4-inch pieces). Cook
~{1-2%minutes}, until fragrant.

Return the chicken, turn the heat to high and stir-fry ~{1%minute}. Stir the
sauce, pour it in and toss ~{1%minute} more, until it thickens and coats.

Toss in the peanuts and serve.
